my name is francois pomare  and i'm a director of research at the  national center for scientific research  in paris  and i'm also adjunct professor  basically counselor advisor to the  institute of language and culture  studies from the royal university of  buda  how did i get there  well i've been working for almost now  35 years in the himalayas  i did my m.a in ladakh  and then i got this wonderful  opportunity to go to bhutan through a  contact  and i thought i would be going for a  month or two so i went with a gin and my  sleeping bag  and 32 years later i'm still there so i  guess this is a long life commitment  at the beginning partnership was  extremely difficult because the  even idea of research as a western idea  was not understood at all and  anthropology was not even existing  so it was very difficult so i did my own  research  while working for the government of  bhutan  and  in tourism and education  and then  slowly slowly it opened  and i now partner completely  with the  institute for language and cultural  studies of the university of bhutan and  i believe in partnership completely  it's my  really my aspiration  is to be useful to help  in my little way the chinese to come  very strong in anthropology and history  about their own culture on the  international scene  and the bhutanese have a great advantage  because education is in english  so their level of english is good  already  the royal university of bhutan is a  collection  of  a federation of colleges about 10  colleges  which are spread out in the whole of the  country so we have a lot of  communication problems  but they chose this model in order to  encourage the development of the local  communities  so if you have a university in local  rural communities it helps the people to  get  a  partnership to get business opportunity  and so on so that was designed for that  the university was created officially  as a federation in 2003 by world charter  and  it has  different  colleges in  arts business  technology  and mathematics physics  and  medical science  but um  the  biggest of the problems is really  communication because internet  communication are still a problem  and so we spend a lot of time traveling  from one place to timpu which is a main  the administration is  now the ilcs institute of language and  culture study was created in 1963  but not under this name by the third  king  and dingo cancer rinpoche  as a way to preserve culture and  language  it was very very monastic in its  approach at the beginning and slowly  over the years it has completely changed  and now it has become  a full-fledged college of the royal  university of bhutan  and it has been shifted from its  previous location which was in temple  and too small  to a very remote area of south of  tungsten central bhutan where there was  nothing  and that literally  cut down the bushes made the road and  now it's a huge campus  we have about  in terms of  space  and we have about 700 students now  and we have a new  uh completely new curriculum  which incorporate a lot of western  science like anthropology cultural  studies  but it has retained  its  traditional  classes like philosophy poetry and also  dances etiquette which is so important  in buddha protocol etiquette whatever  you call it  so it's a absolutely amazing place and  it's a mix it really symbolizes buddha  in that way it's a mix between  our tradition and extremely  modern  contemporary  outlook  you
 

